WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.157
Meet the IRIV IO Controller MQTT.

2
00:00:03.157 --> 00:00:06.634
Wire in your signals, and it publishes them.

3
00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:07.819
No code needed.

4
00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.112
It reads switches, sensors and meters,

5
00:00:11.112 --> 00:00:12.522
and sends each one to your

6
00:00:12.522 --> 00:00:13.933
broker as a clean message.

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.064
At a pump station, a float switch

8
00:00:18.064 --> 00:00:19.968
on input zero reports low water.

9
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.285
The dashboard answers over MQTT, and

10
00:00:23.285 --> 00:00:25.139
output zero starts the pump.

11
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.786
Pressure rises on the analog input,

12
00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:29.432
and each change is published.

13
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.046
Everything wires in at the front: two

14
00:00:33.046 --> 00:00:35.365
terminal blocks, Ethernet and a setup port.

15
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.807
Power is ten to thirty

16
00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:40.465
volts, with surge protection.

17
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.124
Isolated RS485 reads Modbus meters and

18
00:00:45.124 --> 00:00:47.751
sensors, and passes their values on.

19
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:51.122
Two analog inputs take zero to ten

20
00:00:51.122 --> 00:00:53.324
volts, or four to twenty milliamps.

21
00:00:54.983 --> 00:00:57.858
Eleven isolated inputs come in two groups.

22
00:00:57.858 --> 00:01:00.047
The first five share one common.

23
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.063
The other six share a second, so

24
00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.160
sinking and sourcing sensors can mix.

25
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.764
Four isolated outputs switch relays, lamps and valves.

26
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.994
One Ethernet cable carries every

27
00:01:13.994 --> 00:01:15.248
message to your broker.

28
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.973
USB C is for setup and firmware updates.

29
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.600
A light for every input and output

30
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.123
shows its state at a glance.

31
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.131
Side switches set the RS485 terminator, and

32
00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:32.878
voltage or current for each analog input.

33
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:36.107
On a machine, it reports every cycle,

34
00:01:36.107 --> 00:01:37.911
and a stop the moment it happens.

35
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:44.478
Setup is a web page.

36
00:01:44.478 --> 00:01:47.058
Enter your broker, tick the channels,

37
00:01:47.058 --> 00:01:48.871
name the topics, and save.

38
00:01:51.083 --> 00:01:53.161
Inside is a dual core Raspberry Pi

39
00:01:53.161 --> 00:01:55.666
microcontroller, in a fanless metal case.

40
00:01:57.583 --> 00:01:59.364
It clips onto a standard DIN

41
00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:00.955
rail, next to the supply.

42
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:03.786
No scripts.

43
00:02:03.786 --> 00:02:05.646
No syntax errors.

44
00:02:05.646 --> 00:02:08.599
Just your signals, on MQTT.

45
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:12.379
Explore the terminals and compatible parts in the

46
00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:14.680
interactive demo, and build your own system.
